<Base title="About" current="about" reading description="Christian Weilbach: from philosophy and liquid democracy to machine learning and systems that improve themselves.">
  <div class="spread section">
    <div class="main about">
      <h1>About</h1>

      <p class="lede">
        All my life I have asked how things are built and how they evolve, from cells and societies to machines. Different
        theories, perspectives and experiences have deepened my understanding of the question and of what the answers have
        in common. Philosophy, neural networks, probabilistic programs and simulations each let me reason about different
        dimensions of it. Some structure of the process stays the same across all of them, and that structure is what
        interests me most.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the questions come from</h2>
      <p>
        I came to machine learning from philosophy. Hegel taught me to see history as a learning process
        that grows by reflecting on itself. Bogdanov's tektology, Stafford Beer's cybernetics and Heinz von Foerster's
        work describe organisations and observers as learners of this kind. I hold the constructivist view that a learner builds its model of the world through its
	own activity in the form of predictions over latent variables. Critical theory, from German Idealism to Horkheimer, Adorno
        and Žižek, gave me ways to challenge these ideas, in particular to ask whom a rational system serves and which perspectives it hides.
      </p>
      <p>
      While I subscribe to constructivism, I do not think the tension between instrumental, bounded rationality and reflection on purpose 
      can be generally resolved. It returns wherever intelligent organisations emerge, at human level or beyond. Building such systems
      includes building the places where that tension can be resolved into a new unified level of abstraction. I appreciate the understanding developed in science fiction. In particular <cite>Ghost in the Shell</cite> and <cite>Westworld</cite> seem to touch on many issues that are becoming increasingly real with the advent of reasoning models and forms of AGI.
      </p>
      <p>
      I personally deeply enjoy cooperation and trusted environments, but I do not privilege cooperation over competition in principle. Both are important and which of the two serves the people involved depends on the situation, and that is often unclear in advance. But I think that better programming models for self-organisation and management, including simulation, help people examine and explore such situations before they struggle unnecessarily with real consequences.
      </p>

      <h2>From philosophy to programming</h2>
      <p>
        After a year of mathematics and computer science, I studied philosophy and cultural anthropology at Heidelberg
	University from 2006 to 2011. This was foremost a period of critical self-engagement in which I wanted to figure out what is truthful, how this can be known, and increasingly also why the world is organised this way and why there are so many horrible problems we have faced and still face today, including extreme poverty, war and climate change.
        I transformed my fascination for computers and open-source software into computer science studies through <a href="http://reluk.ca/project/Votorola/home-2013.html">Votorola</a>,
        liquid democracy software designed by my friend Michael Allan. We engaged with Occupy and other liquid democracy groups
        between 2007 and 2014. It made me think about forms of democratic coordination and automation that are amenable to collective
        governance and transparent self-optimisation, and in a deep sense I still work on that even though my perspective has been deeply transformed since then through my more general and deeper understanding of the universal computational aspects in all the involved processes.
      </p>
      <p>
      I did not expect computer science to deepen my philosophical understanding that much after my philosophy studies. In practice, Votorola needed a data model that could be distributed across many participants. But when I faced concurrency in the imperative environment of Java I quickly realised I had to find a better way or would not be able to work on the collaborative infrastructure I cared about. That led me to Clojure, distributed systems and persistent data structures in the form of <a href="https://github.com/replikativ">replikativ</a> (2013), and then to my first properly academic project with a
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.05545">paper on conflict resolution in open replication</a> with Konrad Kühne
        and Annette Bieniusa. Eventually this became <a href="https://datahike.io">Datahike</a> and a much wider ecosystem of libraries and projects.
      </p>

      <h2>Learning machines</h2>
      <p>
      During my computer science studies in Heidelberg I could also re-engage with my mathematical curiosity, in particular with statistical inference and how uncertainties interplay with computation. This led me to a minor in computational linguistics, machine learning lectures and ultimately into the Human Brain Project, where I worked in the machine learning group for neuromorphic chips on early deep learning architectures.
      My Bachelor's thesis was on online learning for Boltzmann machines on spiking neuron models, which I extended as a research assistant after my thesis. I then continued with a Master's with focus on scientific computing and machine learning and completed it with my Master's thesis on dictionary learning with Bayesian GANs for few-shot classification. In parallel, I co-organised the Clojure meetup in Mannheim-Heidelberg and the Machine Learning Meetup Rhein-Neckar, and in 2018 co-founded LambdaForge as a cooperative functional programming company to develop Datahike and support it in production.
      </p>
      <p>
        For my PhD I moved to Vancouver and the <a href={thesis.group.url}>PLAI group</a> of
        <a href={thesis.advisor.url}>Frank Wood</a> at the University of British Columbia, where I worked on projecting the
        structure of a problem into the neural network architectures that are trained to solve it.
      </p>

      <h2>History, language and how communities remember</h2>
      <p>
        With my partner and historian <a href="https://gisellegonzalezgarcia.github.io/portfolio/">Dr. Giselle González García</a> I have worked on what language models can do for
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.10808">research in history</a>, and built two open databases of migration on
        this stack, the <a href="https://gisellegonzalezgarcia.github.io/ciid/">Cuba–Ireland Immigration Database</a> and
        <a href="https://gisellegonzalezgarcia.github.io/redes_islenas/">Redes Isleñas</a>, on Canarian emigration to the
        Americas.
      </p>

      <h2>Now</h2>
      <p>
        I am the founder and CEO of <a href="https://simm.is">Simmis</a>, where we build software for organisations of people and AI
        agents. The research on this site continues there and in the open-source projects underneath it.
      </p>
